# AGENTS.md IS NO LONGER IGNORED (2026-08-05, P4c). It was `/AGENTS.md` on the list below, so a
# governing onboarding document -- the one that tells an agent which five files are the safety
# envelope, which gates must pass, and how to register a module -- existed in NO clone. Measured
# before the change: `git check-ignore -v AGENTS.md` -> `.gitignore:76:/AGENTS.md`, and
# `git ls-files --error-unmatch AGENTS.md` -> "did not match any file(s) known to git", i.e. ignored
# AND untracked. Two named gates were blind to it as a direct consequence: tools/citation_guard.py
# scans `git ls-files` only, so nothing it says about this file's citations was ever computed; and
# tools/doc_command_guard.py names `AGENTS.md` LITERALLY in its own SCOPE tuple, so it counts a
# document that no clone has. RE-RUN 2026-08-05 rather than quoted: the gate prints
# `scanned 55 documents; 51 python -m command(s)` on this working machine, and AGENTS.md
# contributes 1 document + 1 command (its `python -m snn_data_lake.brainstem.reflex`; the three
# `python -m ruff` lines do not count -- the gate's CMD regex matches `-m snn_data_lake.<...>`
# only). CORRECTION FOUND BY RE-RUNNING: docs/POLICIES.md states the clean-clone figure as
# "54 / 50". The command count is right and the document count is off by one, because `CLAUDE.md`
# is ALSO in that SCOPE tuple and is ALSO gitignored+untracked (it holds 0 counted commands). A
# clean clone therefore measures 53 / 50, not 54 / 50. THAT SUBTRACTION ONLY HOLDS IF THOSE TWO ARE
# THE ONLY UNCLONABLE DOCUMENTS IN SCOPE, which the first draft asserted without checking. Checked
# 2026-08-05 by walking all 55 SCOPE matches against `git ls-files`: exactly two are absent from it,
# AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md, so 55-2 = 53 documents and 51-1 = 50 commands. (The gate's printed
# command figure is net of its 2 EXEMPT entries -- the raw regex matches 53 -- so the arithmetic is
# done on the printed number throughout.) Under P4c "a build the gates cannot see is
# not a build the project can claim", and that applies to a governing document as hard as to code.
# WHAT THIS CHANGE DID AND DID NOT DO, stated exactly: it made the file ADDABLE. (It did not track
# it; that step was left to the owner, and the owner took it -- AGENTS.md was added and committed in
# bcad89ea on 2026-08-05, so it is now tracked and citation_guard scans it for the first time. The
# two sentences above described the state between those two events and are kept, past-tensed, rather
# than deleted: the sequence is the point, because "un-ignored" and "governed" are different states
# and the gap between them is where a governance file can sit looking fixed while nothing reads it.)
# NOT DISPOSITIONED HERE: the `/CLAUDE.md` PATTERN below is the identical defect -- likewise ignored
# and untracked, 26,756 bytes of project governance (re-measured 2026-08-05). DELIBERATELY CITED BY
# PATTERN AND CARRYING NO LINE NUMBER, which is this block's own correction and was earned twice.
# The first draft wrote `.gitignore:77:/CLAUDE.md`: true at HEAD, already false once this comment was
# inserted above it. The fix for that then wrote the re-measured line instead -- and inserting THAT
# sentence moved the target again, so the replacement was stale before it was saved. A comment
# sitting above the line it cites, in the same file, invalidates its own citation on every edit; the
# only stable reference is the pattern itself. Re-derive with `git check-ignore -v CLAUDE.md`.
# DISPOSITIONED 2026-08-05 (owner decision): CLAUDE.md STAYS IGNORED AND UNTRACKED, deliberately.
# Tracking it would publish 26,756 bytes to a public repo, which is a disclosure decision rather than
# a hygiene one, and the "local-only" marking below is the owner's answer to it. The consequence is
# accepted and written down instead of being left to look like an oversight: its 18 `file.py:NNN`
# citations are checked by NOTHING, because citation_guard scans `git ls-files` and an untracked file
# is not in it. Recorded as a named exception under P4c in docs/POLICIES.md.
# The corresponding gate fix went the OTHER way: tools/doc_command_guard.py globs the filesystem
# rather than git, so it was scanning CLAUDE.md and reporting 55 documents here against 54 in CI and
# in every clone. CLAUDE.md is removed from its SCOPE so the gate measures one corpus everywhere.
# That costs 0 commands (CLAUDE.md contains no `python -m snn_data_lake.<...>` invocations).

# Local-only -- kept on disk, not published to the public repo
/CLAUDE.md
/assets/
/gpu_scaling_sweep.sh
vendor/

# models/ holds trained artifacts that are rebuilt, not reviewed (ar_spiking_lm.npz is 39 MB).
# `models/*` rather than `models/`, because git cannot un-exclude a file inside an excluded
# DIRECTORY -- with `models/` the two negations below would silently do nothing.
models/*

# ...except the two perception packs, which are VENDORED on purpose.
#
# They are 0.72 MB and 1.57 MB. Every reader of them is a live faculty, and without them a
# fresh clone has no perception at all -- which is not hypothetical: `cortex/percept_surprise`
# asserts CANNOT VERIFY when the N-MNIST pack is missing (deliberately, replacing a SKIP that
# scored as PASS), so CI has failed on every run since the assertion landed. The alternative was
# a CI step installing the [nmnist] extra and downloading ~38 MB per job from zenodo and NYU to
# regenerate a 0.72 MB file -- slower, and it makes the build depend on two external hosts
# staying up. training/make_packs.py remains the reproducible producer and regenerates both
# byte-for-byte, so vendoring costs reviewability of nothing: the recipe is in the tree.
!models/nmnist_pack.npz
!models/shd_pack.npz

# ARC-AGI-3 arcade downloads (research/arc_agi/play.py)
environment_files/
recordings/

